counterexample:
A backend validates input shape and checks an authenticated caller's resource permissions, such as self-or-admin profile updates:

```rust
fn update_profile(caller: &Session, user_id: u32, new_bio: String) -> Result<(), AuthError> {
    if caller.user_id != user_id && !caller.is_admin() {
        return Err(AuthError::Unauthorized);
    }
    db.execute("UPDATE profiles SET bio = ? WHERE id = ?", new_bio, user_id);
    Ok(())
}
```

An RPC backend verifies an authenticated end-user context and authorizes the operation rather than trusting a raw user_id:

```rust
fn update_address(ctx: &RequestContext, new_addr: Address) {
    let user_id = ctx.verify_end_user_context().unwrap();
    db::write_address(user_id, new_addr);
}
```

Checks already performed by trusted backend middleware or policy enforcement are not absent merely because the handler lacks inline checks.
